,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":121.047188087,"data":"Problem: A train departs with 100 passengers. At the first stop, 20 leave and 35 enter. How many passengers now?\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":121.047191644,"data":"\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":121.047195015,"data":"[SymboLM Generation]\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":124.039994186,"data":"Okay, so I'm trying to figure out how many passengers are on the train after the first stop. Let me start by breaking down the problem step by step. \r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":124.040042583,"data":"\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":126.880787679,"data":"First, the train departs with 100 passengers. That's straightforward. Now, at the first stop, 20 passengers leave, and 35 new passengers enter. I need to calculate the total number of passengers after these changes.\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":126.880821248,"data":"\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":130.944427442,"data":"Let me write this down to keep track. Initially, there are 100 passengers. Then, 20 leave, so I subtract 20 from 100. That gives me 80 passengers remaining. But then, 35 new passengers board the train, so I add 35 to the current number of passengers. \r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":130.944467147,"data":"\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":134.849567808,"data":"So, 80 plus 35 equals 115. That means after the first stop, there are 115 passengers on the train. I think that makes sense because the train started with 100, lost 20, and gained 35, which should result in 115 passengers in total.\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":134.849601845,"data":"\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":136.321926109,"data":"I don't see any other factors mentioned in the problem, so I don't need to consider anything else like waiting times or other stops\r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":136.321966626,"data":"\r\n"}
